ficulty communicating), D (arrogant). Responsibility: A (leadership), B (conscientious), C (reliable), D (takes responsibility seriously). Adaptability: A (not open to criticism), B (grumpy), C (not willing to further education), D (arrogant). So all have concerns.\n\nWhich candidate is strongest overall? Candidate C has 100% reliability, calm in crisis, correct decisions quickly, technical competence, but communication issues and egocentric, not willing to further education. Candidate B has reliability (conscientious), stress handling, technical (weather, computer), but communication issues (nasty remarks) and weak memory for numbers. Candidate D has responsibility, problem solving, concentration, but arrogance, hot temper, loner. Candidate A has anticipation, complex connections, spatial vision, leadership, but not open to criticism, show-off, unhealthy habits.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. Could we argue that Candidate C's reliability and calmness in crisis outweigh communication issues, making them the best? Or argue that Candidate B's stress handling and reliability are more critical, and communication can be coached. Or propose Candidate D due to responsibility and problem solving.\n\nGiven the panel's current leading candidate is B, we could either support or propose alternative. Let's consider the missing criteria: stress resilience for C is known (keeps calm in crisis). Decision quality for C is strong (correct decisions quickly). Attention for C is strong (excellent attention skills). Crew cooperation for C is poor (egocentric, difficulty communicating). Professional communication also poor. Adaptability also poor (not willing to further education). Responsibility is high (reliable). So C is strong on many technical criteria but weak on crew cooperation and adaptability.\n\nCandidate B: reliability (conscientious), stress handling (handles stress well), technical (weather assessment, computer), but communication issues (nasty remarks), weak memory for num